---
import ContentLayout from "../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { HOME } from "../../utils/breadcrumbs";
---

<ContentLayout
  title="About DataDank"
  description="DataDank is a structured learning and interview-preparation site for Data Engineers. Learn what it covers, how content is reviewed and how evidence is labelled."
  crumbs={[HOME, { label: "About" }]}
  eyebrow="About"
  deck="A learning system for Data Engineers, organised as paths rather than a pile of articles."
  indexable
>
  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>
        DataDank brings learning paths, technology guides, interview preparation,
        system-design case studies and projects into one structure. Every page points to a
        prerequisite, related ideas and a next step.
      </p>
      <h2>Launch focus</h2>
      <p>
        The site starts with Data Engineering only: SQL, Python, Spark, warehousing, lakehouse,
        streaming and orchestration. Other data careers will be added only once Data Engineering
        content is complete and useful.
      </p>
      <h2>Who runs DataDank</h2>
      <p>
        DataDank is an independent project with a single maintainer. It is not affiliated with,
        sponsored by or endorsed by any of the companies or vendors whose tools it teaches, and it
        has no paid placements or affiliate links.
      </p>
      <h2>How content is written and reviewed</h2>
      <p>
        Pages are written to answer one clear question. Technical claims are checked against
        official documentation, which is linked in each page's sources. Runnable SQL, Python and
        PySpark examples are executed before publishing, and the page states the versions they ran
        on. Where behaviour depends on a version or platform release, the page says so. Each page
        shows when it was last reviewed, and that date only changes after a substantive update.
      </p>
      <p>
        AI tools help with outlines, drafting and wording. They do not replace running the examples,
        checking the documentation or editorial review, and no page is described as expert-reviewed
        unless that review has happened.
      </p>
      <h2>Practice problems and external links</h2>
      <p>
        DataDank's explanations and solutions are its own. Where a problem also exists on a judge
        site such as LeetCode or DataLemur, the page links to it so you can run your solution
        against their tests; their problem statements, test cases and editorials are not copied.
      </p>
      <h2>How company interview material is labelled</h2>
      <p>
        Company guides distinguish three kinds of information: verified or attributed facts that
        cite a public source, reported items that cite a named candidate account, and representative
        practice questions that we wrote ourselves. Practice questions are never presented as
        questions a company actually asked.
      </p>
      <h2>Report a problem</h2>
      <p>
        Found a factual error, outdated behaviour or a broken link? Email the address on the
        <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> with the page address and, if possible, a link to the
        documentation that shows the correct behaviour. Confirmed errors are fixed on the page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ContentLayout>
